#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167876} :activity/id #uuid "63363687-eb8d-5f15-94d7-09950a4b9da4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049289 :topic/id #uuid "ea2533e0-c4a4-5c4e-b20a-9fb6ea3d7c70" :topic/math-academy-id 3231} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168015 :tutorial/content "The Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model with **carrying capacity** is given by \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, \n\n- $x=x(t)$ is the *prey population*, and\n\n- $y=y(t)$ is the *predator population*.\n\nThe only difference from the Lotka–Volterra model is the factor $(1-hx)$ in the prey equation. Notice that if $h=0,$ we get the standard model:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our modified version of the model, the *intrinsic growth* of prey is given by the $ax(1-hx)$ term. Now, if there are no predators ($y=0$), then \n\n$$\nx' = ax(1-hx).\n$$\n\nRecall that general solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,\\frac{1}{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{1}{h}.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the *carrying capacity* (the limit the number of prey is approaching as $t$ goes to infinity).\n\n![](images/8b/8b7a6d12af77f6c2d2e6d3596e71a51a3d697a5c28e9f9c64c5675a01df812d5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d7c622bc-33ae-5782-8c5f-cb14af0d21e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14542 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fbe051e-af21-5ed1-a3d3-ed273a110dd2" :step/math-academy-id 590160 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167877}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166561 :question/id #uuid "c8159388-bbcc-5204-8ed3-e9c2b9157d59" :question/math-academy-id "e-21739"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050600}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480698}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"} :step/id #uuid "97f6d26b-a78b-51a8-831e-9c9c2fe5d4d1" :step/math-academy-id 590161 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166558 :question/id #uuid "b7cba6bc-39b5-5e03-ba33-c1fc2a1b4ae8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497359}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"} :step/id #uuid "4ee40fef-c655-575b-86ae-0bef94e43fec" :step/math-academy-id 590164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168016 :tutorial/content "Recall that the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas equilibria at\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nNotice that the second component of the third equilibrium point is positive only when \n\n$$\nh < \\dfrac{d}{c},\n$$\n\nwhich guarantees that the third fixed point will lie in the first quadrant. For now, let's consider only this case.\n\nWe now evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/89/89ca57d2d308201a46a31695c27ad866604ef4f230609ad3047e186824e5dc28.svg)\nat the nonzero equilibrium ![](images/f2/f2965d33d48e48fa977e876803aa3da55c1c47c3569a24cb8341b97ba43420c2.svg) to get\n\n![](images/b2/b239f1170a7bf5a5f6ad6043492aa0772adcc2a53e7f3ebdad15ad195b1520d9.svg)\n\nBecause we are considering the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -a < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nAnalyzing the type of the third equilibrium in full generality becomes considerably more cumbersome. Therefore, we'll examine a concrete example later in the lesson. For now, we simply note that the third equilibrium is an *asymptotically stable (spiral) sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c82332ed5277586a2b97e900383619a5d61cdb73ff4b60a92de3d5460165165.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7673170825344e1831f2ba01ce4c20f10091380ff4e116f3b6bd9e7666e51aa.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ce000cd-7913-5e8e-90d2-3a5a820db4f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14543 :tutorial/title "Local Behavior Near the Nonzero Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "b61ca7e0-a217-5555-8d14-3f0d56453cb5" :step/math-academy-id 590167 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166559 :question/id #uuid "3e280ec5-c95a-5ca6-ba2e-edc4cf653ea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4936474f-5e5f-5da2-8242-1b48336a836c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048881}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497388}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Stable Case"} :step/id #uuid "33bb23bb-bd23-5d38-9487-0ff5ffdddc57" :step/math-academy-id 590168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168017 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas equilibria at\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nWe have already considered the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ which guarantees that the third equilibrium will lie in the first quadrant. Let's now consider the remaining cases.\n\n- When $h = \\dfrac{d}{c},$ the third equilibrium coincides with the second one. So, we have only two equilibria:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, 0\\right)\n$$\n\n- When $h > \\dfrac{d}{c},$ the third equilibrium is in the fourth quadrant (its $y$-coordinate is negative). So, we have only two equilibria that make sense in the context of the model:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nHence, we get only two equilibria. \n\nThe trajectories near the origin remain similar. However, the equilibrium at\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nwill be an *asymptotically stable sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h \\geq \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b94beebb63bc1312d56f655e48b3bc7cdb1b9badc5cdacd6b88b073c5055adc9.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right).$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd60f32a5daff3c805b097d121814d19e04781ed1bcfead989cde82db674e5d9.png)\n\nEventually, the *predators go extinct* while the prey approaches the carrying capacity of the system.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "92ed0e52-b2f2-5d81-99c3-60526aa8f13a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14547 :tutorial/title "Predator Extinction Case"} :step/id #uuid "0b301d8f-79ee-5a20-babe-fd63b54fd69b" :step/math-academy-id 590172 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166560 :question/id #uuid "3076e962-385d-5c19-8179-7c1a91aa6e55" :question/math-academy-id "e-21738"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b87aeba5-eb67-5dc0-9c25-d9c1cefdb6e2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497413}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Predator Extinction Case"} :step/id #uuid "51d64692-6d2f-5189-bd9c-6590b8925293" :step/math-academy-id 590173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168028 :tutorial/content "Recall that the equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, for the Lotka–Volterra model \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe equilibria are the solutions of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)-bxy=0 \\\\ -cy+dxy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+dxy & =0 \\\\ y(-c+dx) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=\\dfrac{c}{d}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)-bx(0) & =0 \\\\ ax(1-hx) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$\n\n- If $x=\\dfrac{c}{d},$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{c}{d})(1-h(\\frac{c}{d}))-b(\\frac{c}{d})y & =0 \\\\ a(1-\\frac{c}{d}h)-by & =0 \\\\ a(1-\\frac{c}{d}h) & =by \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{b}(1-\\frac{c}{d}h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1-\\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1-\\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79e78f76-a29e-544f-a86b-9f714e69cf63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14597 :tutorial/title "Equilibrium Points"} :step/id #uuid "f7c2a3b7-bd87-5cba-ba5e-ea5beeed7071" :step/math-academy-id 590177 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168029 :tutorial/content "Consider the modified Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants, $x=x(t)$ is the prey population, and $y=y(t)$ is the predator population.\n\nTo classify the equilibrium at the origin, we need to find the corresponding Jacobian matrix.\n\nLet's denote $f_1(x,y)=ax(1-hx)-bxy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-cy+dxy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(ax(1\\,-\\,hx)-bxy)=a(1\\,-\\,2hx)-by, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(ax(1\\,-\\,hx)-bxy)=-bx, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-cy+dxy)=dy, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-cy+dxy)=-c+dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/1d/1db459c6fd9f3bad4e5f044d8de8a4e185aeecadddcd73fac75dff09e6fa607d.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/03/035a074e405004b43a3f5e509b46affa6e0e53dbf53e26e01149b7ae545c6f64.svg) to get\n\n![](images/cf/cf1eb1b02f6d0dd1b690ac3a69abd1ac6777f592c713060920ef1dcee3fb5600.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = a > 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = -c < 0.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nThus, the equilibrium at the origin for this model behaves identically to that of the standard Lotka–Volterra model." :tutorial/id #uuid "feef6e7a-79a2-5b71-8cec-dc45e305ec52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14598 :tutorial/title "Local Behavior Near the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "8118c930-2041-5a97-bb4f-5812d27ead9b" :step/math-academy-id 590178 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168030 :tutorial/content "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-0.25x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll evaluate its Jacobian at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[4, \\, 0]^T$ that lies on the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote $f_1(x,y)=4x(1-0.25x)-xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-2y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-0.25x)-xy)=4-2x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-0.25x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+3xy)=-2+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/0b/0bd1edc4527b82bebf84d38f8d4a79b65062d6b38c5fa4c84b7509acaa547f42.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/30/30463f939876ccbee7cf77f8fdc80fb4e8a27fe0ad5af22cd51e1be3433a3af5.svg) to get\n\n![](images/fc/fc40e7e568705e08b70e0dedae710b2e8514837491730ae12a90bed764261f97.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = 10$ and $\\lambda_2=-4$. Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nLet's now see an example with the equilibrium that lies in the first quadrant (the third equilibrium)." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f65e770-de67-59cc-9bdf-6bac2b6bc60e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14599 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example for the Equilibrium on the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "ca9f5992-2b5d-50f0-b691-0284aafa5f4f" :step/math-academy-id 590179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167880}}] :activity/title "The Lotka-Volterra Model With Carrying Capacity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167005}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497473} :activity/id #uuid "7dbc97d0-d2e5-5a31-9865-e4bc3b4b7c2e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049289 :topic/id #uuid "ea2533e0-c4a4-5c4e-b20a-9fb6ea3d7c70" :topic/math-academy-id 3231} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e91b260-25e1-5b47-906c-3f64582c0332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496685} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb3bef2-2545-5bc4-8969-c1f6f768c65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496794} {:answer/id #uuid "464a739e-453b-5804-827e-c865d0223c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496810} {:answer/id #uuid "b74ad0ea-7a92-5fdf-853c-e5ea3b383b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497035} {:answer/id #uuid "f7608866-ec95-56fe-a9c6-3418baf703e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496794} :answer-field/id #uuid "00fdc5c7-d728-5093-9485-058b327df4f0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497208} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13374a74-507a-5c1f-af88-e501c2d0fa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496701} {:answer/id #uuid "73e29e26-144e-5ad1-b72a-20664d8d828a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496904} {:answer/id #uui